WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.425
Here is one lambda, in Java and in Kotlin.

2
00:00:05.425 --> 00:00:07.566
It captures fee and adds it to x.

3
00:00:07.566 --> 00:00:13.600
You get an object that implements an interface, yet the compiler writes no class file for it.

4
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:23.236
javac turns the lambda into two things: an invokedynamic instruction where the lambda was, and a private static method that holds its body.

5
00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:27.719
The body method takes the captured fee first, then the lambda's own x.

6
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:30.243
Read the call site type as a factory.

7
00:00:30.243 --> 00:00:34.176
Give it the captured int, and it gives you an IntUnaryOperator.

8
00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:37.835
Other invoke instructions name the method they call.

9
00:00:37.835 --> 00:00:43.971
invokedynamic names only a name, a type, and a bootstrap method that will choose the target.

10
00:00:43.971 --> 00:00:48.573
For a lambda, that is LambdaMetafactory, with three static arguments.

11
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.047
The first time the instruction runs, the JVM calls the bootstrap method.

12
00:00:54.047 --> 00:01:01.211
It passes a Lookup, which carries your class's access rights, plus the name, the type and the static arguments.

13
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:06.080
LambdaMetafactory spins a hidden class that implements IntUnaryOperator.

14
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.629
It's a nestmate of your class, so it may call the private body method.

15
00:01:10.833 --> 00:01:19.293
It returns a ConstantCallSite holding a method handle, a typed reference to a method that the JVM can call directly.

16
00:01:19.293 --> 00:01:21.919
The JVM links the instruction to it.

17
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:27.144
Every later run calls that target directly, and the bootstrap method never runs again.

18
00:01:27.144 --> 00:01:31.073
The target can't change, so the JIT can inline straight through it.

19
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:37.305
This lambda captures fee, so each time the expression runs you get a new small object.

20
00:01:37.305 --> 00:01:39.076
All of them share one class.

21
00:01:39.076 --> 00:01:42.617
A lambda that captures nothing gets one shared instance.

22
00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:48.159
Kotlin compiles a lambda the same way, by default since Kotlin 2.0.

23
00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:52.461
Its interface is Function1, whose invoke is erased to Object.

24
00:01:52.461 --> 00:01:58.173
So the third bootstrap argument tells the metafactory to insert boxing and casts.

25
00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:01.051
A Kotlin function reference is different.

26
00:02:01.051 --> 00:02:05.633
In the lesson's demo it still compiled to an ordinary generated class.

27
00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:07.660
Here is a check.

28
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:11.611
plus is called 1,000 times with 1,000 different fees.

29
00:02:11.611 --> 00:02:15.041
How many lambda classes, and how many objects?

30
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:22.676
One class, linked once at that call site, and 1,000 objects, because each one captures its own fee.
